    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d60">

                <pb facs="0078" n="66" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg66"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.5911/388½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. Hugh S. dimming, Jr., of the Division
                        of European Affairs</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-06-24">June 24, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Blechingberg telephoned me this morning and said that he had at last received
                    instructions from the Foreign Office in Copenhagen to leave the United States as
                    soon as possible and to take Mr. Möller and Mr. Tscherning with him. After
                    consulting Mr. Atherton<anchor n="27" xml:id="d60-f1"/> I telephoned Mr.
                    Blechingberg and asked him to call on me at the State Department this
                    afternoon.</p>
                <p>Mr. Blechingberg read me a telegram which he had received from the Foreign
                    Office. In substance it said that in view of existing circumstances the Foreign
                    Office desired him and the two former Danish consular officers to leave the
                    United States as quickly as possible and directed him to inform the Foreign
                    Office of the route chosen by him and the pertinent dates.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
